RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) ties a resource's lifetime to an object's lifetime. The resource is acquired in the constructor and released in the destructor. This guarantees cleanup even if an exception is thrown. Classic example:

```cpp
class FileHandle {
    FILE* f;
public:
    FileHandle(const char* path) { f = fopen(path, "r"); }
    ~FileHandle() { if (f) fclose(f); }
};
```

`std::unique_ptr`, `std::lock_guard`, `std::vector` are all RAII. It eliminates entire classes of resource leaks.
